<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theresienstadt Ghetto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1941–1945</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Terezín Ghetto</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Theresienstadt Ghetto was a Nazi ghetto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camp</a> in the fortress town of Terezín in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, in use from November 1941 to May 1945. Presented in propaganda as a model Jewish settlement, it held about 140,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, mainly from Bohemia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Over 30,000 died there, and most of the others were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> and other killing centres.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theresienstadt-ghetto.jpg" alt="Theresienstadt Ghetto" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bedřich Fritta · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:In_the_Living_Quarters.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
